==English==

===Etymology===
Derivative of 17th-century vernacular {{m|en|bam||to trick, to con}}, which is a derivative of {{m|en|bam}} in noun use (fraudster, cheat). Possibly from {{cog|fr|embobiner}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bæm.ˈbuː.zl̩/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bamboozle.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[con]], [[defraud]], [[trick]], to [[make a fool of]], to [[humbug]] or [[impose]] on someone.
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 19|chapter 19]]|text=“Look here, friend,” said I, “if you have anything important to tell us, out with it; but if you are only trying to '''bamboozle''' us, you are mistaken in your game; that’s all I have to say.”}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[confuse]], [[frustrate]] or [[perplex]].
#: {{ux|en|He's completely '''bamboozled''' by the changes in the computer system.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=February|magazine=Chess Review|title=Chess Caviar|passage="Although Morphy misplays the opening, it does not take him long to '''bamboozle''' his opponent."}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=October 12 2021|author=Jamie Lyall|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/58793557|work=BBC Sport|title=Faroe Islands 0-1 Scotland|passage={{w|Steve Clarke|Clarke}}'s defence, bolstered by the return of {{w|Grant Hanley}}, was '''bamboozled''' by a flurry of Faroese raids, at least one of which should have delivered a goal.}}

